            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we had identified various locations where excess air leakage &amp; heat loss occurs:



** There was significant air leakage coming from the back side of the chimney, where it passes up through the basement ceiling. In fact, this space appears to be open all the way up into the ceiling cavity above the main floor. The heat that escapes up this opening is likely the main culprit that is causing the extensive Ice Damming you are experiencing along the front &amp; back of your roof ........ pretty much in line with where the chimney is !!



** Please keep in mind the current co-vented chimney can be completely eliminated if you upgrade to a high efficiency, direct-vent furnace &amp; on-demand hot water appliances.



Savings: A modest 10% reduction in air leakage is estimated to save $ 24 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.9 GJ. For your house, with all of the air leakage "heat loss" associated with your chimney, I estimate your annual energy savings to be 3 or 4 times that amount.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV)



Your house currently has adequate ventilation, however a lot of it comes from the excessive air leakage going up and around the chimney. It appears the heat escaping up into ceiling attic space has been causing you some significant Ice Damming on the front &amp; back sections of your roof.



Once this air leakage is removed, you may find that your house could become "under-ventilated". Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air.



This can be corrected in a few ways:



1.      If you update the furnace &amp; hot water tank to high efficiency, direct-vent equipment, you can have the current 5" combustion air pipe converted to become a 5" fresh air pipe ....... directly connected to the return air ductwork of your furnace (with a manual adjustment damper). Each time the furnace comes on, so outside fresh air will be circulated throughout the house.



2.      In addition to #1 above, you could have a timer or humidity sensing switch connected to your main floor bathroom fan to increase ventilation. Each time the bathroom fan operates, fresh air will be drawn in through the 5" fresh air supply pipe identified in #1 above.



3.      Rather than using #1 &amp; #2 above, your heating &amp; ventilation contractor could install a 75% efficient heat recovery ventilator that operates on a "20 minute on &amp; 40 minute off" cycle - providing about 30 CFM of fresh air when it operates.



If you choose #3 above, I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, the annual cost should be more than offset by the savings you will achieve from eliminating all of the air leakage through &amp; around the current chimney.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre propane-fired hot water tank (that was just installed this year), co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. If you decide to upgrade your furnace to a high efficiency, direct-vent propane model, you should consider installing a new on-demand propane hot water heater - this will allow you to completely eliminate the current chimney. You should consider replacing the current hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 61 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.6 GJ and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an oil furnace that is 83% efficient and it co-vents (together with the hot water tank) up a chimney. As this chimney is not well sealed &amp; causing you ice damming issues, I'm wondering if this is just a good time to eliminate that chimney altogether by upgrading both your furnace &amp; hot water appliances to high efficiency propane direct vent units. I have modeled an upgrade from this current oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 855 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one on the main floor is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting (your house has mostly these older inefficient lights), and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Flourescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood vents inside, however the 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer vent outside), indicated a pressure drop of 4.2 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; hot water tank vent up a chimney are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I do not recall seeing any smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - Both bathroom fans vent to the outside

        - The clothes dryer vents to the outside



NOTE: Your house is currently depending upon the 5" combustion air pipe to help supplement the ventilation of this house.



If you choose to upgrade the furnace and hot water tank to high efficiency, direct-vent equipment (thereby eliminating the chimney), you should keep the current 5" combustion air pipe, however have it connected to the "return air ductwork" of the furnace (as an outside fresh air supply) to help bring in &amp; distribute fresh air throughout the house each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan in the house is turned on.



The heating contractor will install an integrated manual damper so you can adjust the amount of fresh air entering the house. Open it up further if the house is humid with condensation on the windows &amp; lingering odours, and close it down further if the house feels dry, with lots of static electricity.



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.38 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.03 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.62 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.09 per bag



